```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc11
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a duplicate IP address on pc11. The device's startup script correctly
assigns 192.168.200.11/24, but at runtime pc11's eth0 carries 192.168.200.12/24
(the same address as pc12). This means both pc11 (MAC 02:33:4d:f4:a2:be) and pc12
(MAC b6:3b:13:9a:47:14) simultaneously claim ownership of 192.168.200.12 on the
shared switch5/switch4 L2 segment.

Whenever any device (router4 gateway, a remote host, etc.) ARPs for 192.168.200.12,
both pc11 and pc12 reply with their own MAC addresses. Whichever ARP reply arrives
last wins the ARP cache entry. Because the two replies race continuously and each
gratuitous ARP further overwrites the cache, the recorded MAC for 192.168.200.12
alternates between the two values — producing the observed "ARP flipping." Pings
succeed when the ARP cache happens to point to pc12, and fail (or reach the wrong
host) when it points to pc11.

A secondary consequence is that 192.168.200.11 has no owner at all: router4 shows
it INCOMPLETE, so pc11's intended address is entirely unreachable.

Root cause: pc11's IP address was changed post-boot from 192.168.200.11/24 to
192.168.200.12/24, duplicating pc12's address.

Corrective fix: On pc11, remove the incorrect address and restore the intended one:
  ip addr del 192.168.200.12/24 dev eth0
  ip addr add 192.168.200.11/24 dev eth0
  ip route add default via 192.168.200.1
This eliminates the duplicate, stops the ARP thrashing, and restores 192.168.200.11
as a reachable address.
=== END ===
```